//kage:unit pixels

package main

// The dual-Kawase downsample pass of the Full glass level.
//
// # Why downsampling is the blur
//
// The project plan, section 8, makes the argument and this shader is it: a
// Gaussian of radius 16 needs 33 taps, separated 2x33, and at 1080p that is
// not affordable on a VideoCore VI. At 1/8 resolution a small kernel behaves
// like a large radius at full resolution, because each level has already
// averaged everything between its taps. Dual-Kawase spends a handful of taps
// per level over a chain whose total area is a fraction of the material
// region — not of the screen.
//
// # Ebitengine samples nearest, always, and that changes the kernel
//
// The textbook dual-Kawase down pass is five taps and leans on the bilinear
// filter to average the four texels each of them lands between. Ebitengine
// does not offer that to a Kage shader. Its OpenGL backend sets
// GL_TEXTURE_MIN_FILTER and GL_TEXTURE_MAG_FILTER to GL_NEAREST once, in
// internal/graphicsdriver/opengl/context.go, and never changes them;
// DrawTrianglesShaderOptions has no Filter field at all, because linear
// filtering for the built-in draw path is generated into the built-in shader
// rather than configured on the sampler. So imageSrc0At is a texel fetch.
//
// Written against a nearest sampler, the five tap kernel silently throws half
// the source away: the centre tap of a two-to-one downsample lands exactly on
// the boundary between two texels, and which one it picks is up to the driver.
// The result aliases on any high contrast edge, and it aliases *differently*
// depending on the level, which is the worst kind of blur artefact because it
// crawls when the content scrolls.
//
// So the centre tap here is written out as the 2x2 box it was always supposed
// to be — four fetches at the four texel centres the destination texel covers,
// which is exactly what a bilinear fetch at the boundary would have returned
// and is exact rather than approximate. The four diagonal taps stay single
// fetches at texel centres two texels out. Eight fetches, no aliasing, and no
// dependency on a sampler state this package cannot set.
//
// # Vertex layout
//
//	src0Pos  source position plus the source target's atlas origin
//	custom.x tap scale, normally 1
//	custom.y valid source width  in pixels
//	custom.z valid source height in pixels
//	custom.w unused
//
// No uniforms, for the reason glass.kage states at length.
//
// # Why every tap is clamped
//
// The intermediate targets are pooled and bucketed to a multiple of 64 pixels,
// so a target is normally larger than the level written into it and the
// surplus holds whatever the previous tenant left there. An unclamped tap at
// the right hand edge would drag that in, and it would look exactly like a
// real artefact of the blur.

func tap(p vec2, origin vec2, size vec2) vec4 {
	return imageSrc0At(clamp(p, vec2(0.5), size-vec2(0.5)) + origin)
}

func Fragment(dstPos vec4, src0Pos vec2, color vec4, custom vec4) vec4 {
	origin := imageSrc0Origin()
	o := custom.x
	size := custom.yz
	// The destination texel centre maps to an even source coordinate, which
	// is the corner of the 2x2 block it covers.
	p := src0Pos - origin

	box := tap(p+vec2(-0.5, -0.5), origin, size)
	box += tap(p+vec2(0.5, -0.5), origin, size)
	box += tap(p+vec2(-0.5, 0.5), origin, size)
	box += tap(p+vec2(0.5, 0.5), origin, size)

	d := 2.5 * o
	diag := tap(p+vec2(-d, -d), origin, size)
	diag += tap(p+vec2(d, -d), origin, size)
	diag += tap(p+vec2(-d, d), origin, size)
	diag += tap(p+vec2(d, d), origin, size)

	// box is a sum of four, so box/4 is the centre value and the classic
	// weighting of four for the centre against one each for the diagonals is
	// box + diag over eight.
	return (box + diag) / 8.0
}
